WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.980
This is the Ilulissat Icefjord.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:03.980
Miles upstream,

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:07.899
behind millions of tons of floating ice, is
the Jakobshavn Glacier.

00:00:07.899 --> 00:00:09.150
And if the Greenland ice sheet melts,

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:12.679
a good bit of the water will be coming down
this way.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:14.440
But a glacier isn’t a solid thing,

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.970
it’s not some monolithic block of ice that’s
steadily melting.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.180
Glaciers flow.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.360
It’s a really counterintuitive concept,

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.610
because most of the world is used to thinking
of ice in terms of frozen lakes,

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:25.840
where the ice isn’t going anywhere.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.710
And let's be honest, this doesn’t look like
it’s moving.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:32.279
But glaciers aren’t static: they gain mass
by having snow fall on them,

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:35.079
and they lose mass by a process called calving:

00:00:35.079 --> 00:00:38.429
the front falls off and generates a stream
of icebergs like this.

00:00:38.429 --> 00:00:41.179
Like a river, the glacier flows downhill.

00:00:41.179 --> 00:00:45.120
And the Jakobshavn glacier is one of the fastest
and most productive in the world:

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.289
at the terminus, where the front falls off,

00:00:47.289 --> 00:00:48.920
it can move at 20 metres a day.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.670
And remember, that doesn’t mean the front
of the ice

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:55.649
is moving towards us at 20 metres a day, just
that on average,

00:00:55.649 --> 00:00:57.809
the front bit of the ice is moving at that
speed

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:00.300
when it falls off and forms these icebergs.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.949
If a glacier is gaining mass, it will steadily
advance,

00:01:03.949 --> 00:01:07.220
spreading out into the sea beyond until enough
of the front falls off

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:08.560
that it reaches equilibrium.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.150
And if it’s losing mass,

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:13.110
because there’s not enough snow, or because
it’s melting more quickly,

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:14.150
it will steadily retreat.

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.900
Remember, that’s not ice actually going
into reverse,

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.250
it’s still flowing the same,

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:22.170
it’s just that the point where the front
falls off is moving backwards.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:23.940
It's still producing icebergs.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.700
And the Jakobshavn glacier there?

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:27.850
It produces a lot of icebergs.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:31.810
There’s a decent chance that the one that
sank the Titanic came down here.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.060
Now, I don’t want to debate climate change.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.400
Some very shrill people in the comments probably
will though,

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.740
so I’ll say this: sure, the data is noisy.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:43.700
From the 1960s to 2000, the actual front of
the glacier here barely moved —

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.090
and then in ten years, it retreated about
the same amount

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.810
as it had in the entire 20th century.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.740
But climate change is about trends, not events,

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:54.860
and if you don’t believe the enormous mountain
of evidence by now,

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:58.600
then someone with no earth science credentials
talking into a camera in the Arctic

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.590
isn’t going to convince you either.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.430
And part of the reason for that is that it’s
difficult to get across

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.509
the sheer scale of what I’m seeing here.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:09.909
The camera can’t show you it.

00:02:09.909 --> 00:02:12.760
Even standing here in person,

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:17.359
my brain is refusing to accept just how massive
everything is.

00:02:17.359 --> 00:02:20.769
And we're only seeing the 10, 20% of these
above water.

00:02:20.769 --> 00:02:24.890
Some glaciers will produce blocks of ice literally
the size of Manhattan.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:26.200
And yes, they’ll break up, yes,

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.280
they’ll settle down to be something we’re
more used to,

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.930
but like so many processes on our planet,

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:34.090
this is on a scale that is pretty much unimaginable
to humans.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:38.139
It’s no wonder that the details of climate
change are difficult to understand:

00:02:38.139 --> 00:02:41.269
I’m standing here, watching the ice slowly
flow,

00:02:41.269 --> 00:02:44.439
and I still can’t get my head around this.

00:02:44.439 --> 00:02:48.700
I'm here with Chris Hadfield's Generator Arctic,
and so's she!

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:49.700
Hello!

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.599
That's Elmo Keep, she's writing wonderful
profiles

00:02:53.599 --> 00:02:55.540
of some of the people we meet out here.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:56.959
Follow her on Twitter to see when they're
published.

00:02:56.959 --> 00:02:57.459
Goodbye!

